1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:07,409
This is a walkthrough of a small LLM serving platform I built on Google Kubernetes Engine in one day.

2
00:00:07,409 --> 00:00:13,347
It runs open-weights Qwen models with llama.cpp on a private GKE cluster.

3
00:00:13,347 --> 00:00:18,464
Everything you'll see was measured, including the parts that didn't work the way I expected.

4
00:00:20,078 --> 00:00:21,525
First, the scope.

5
00:00:21,525 --> 00:00:24,246
This is a lab, not a production system.

6
00:00:24,246 --> 00:00:32,020
It runs on CPU only, on spot e2-standard-4 nodes, because GPU quota wasn't available on the day.

7
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:39,780
The models are small: Qwen 2.5 at half a billion and one and a half billion parameters, quantized to 4 bits.

8
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:48,569
The goals were to deploy a model on GKE properly, swap model versions under load, test autoscaling, and write down exactly what happened.

9
00:00:50,186 --> 00:00:56,177
Here's everything that exists in the project, taken from gcloud and kubectl listings.

10
00:00:56,177 --> 00:01:04,003
Terraform created the network: a custom VPC with one subnet and secondary ranges for pods and services.

11
00:01:04,003 --> 00:01:14,172
It also created the GKE cluster, the spot node pool, an Artifact Registry repository, a versioned Cloud Storage bucket, and two service accounts.

12
00:01:14,172 --> 00:01:27,805
GKE generated the rest from configuration: three firewall rules, and the whole load balancer, meaning a forwarding rule, a URL map, backend services, health checks, and a network endpoint group.

13
00:01:27,805 --> 00:01:31,724
One Cloud Build run imported the images and the models.

14
00:01:31,724 --> 00:01:36,125
There's no Cloud NAT, no other virtual machines, and no Cloud Run.

15
00:01:36,125 --> 00:01:47,675
Two things exist that I didn't create: the default VPC, whose firewall allows SSH and RDP from anywhere, and the default Compute Engine service account with the Editor role.

16
00:01:47,675 --> 00:01:51,021
The lab uses neither, and both should be removed.

17
00:01:52,646 --> 00:01:54,666
This is how the pieces fit together.

18
00:01:54,666 --> 00:01:59,926
On the left is the internet: the public model and image sources, and a client.

19
00:01:59,926 --> 00:02:07,804
Inside the project there's a load balancer built by the GKE Gateway, and a VPC with no route to the internet.

20
00:02:07,804 --> 00:02:14,249
Inside that VPC is a private GKE cluster running two llama.cpp pods.

21
00:02:14,249 --> 00:02:25,707
Blue arrows are the request path, green arrows the import path, and the dotted grey lines show how nodes and pods reach the registry and the bucket through identity, not keys.

22
00:02:27,314 --> 00:02:29,021
Let's follow one request.

23
00:02:29,021 --> 00:02:32,849
The client sends a POST to the gateway's public IP address.

24
00:02:32,849 --> 00:02:40,480
That address belongs to a global external Application Load Balancer that GKE created from the Gateway object.

25
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:43,683
The HTTPRoute becomes the URL map.

26
00:02:43,683 --> 00:02:49,907
Only slash v1 and slash health are routed, so the root path returns 404.

27
00:02:49,907 --> 00:02:59,529
The backend service has a 120-second timeout, because generation is slower than the 30-second default, and 45 seconds of connection draining.

28
00:02:59,529 --> 00:03:10,649
Its backend is a container-NATive network endpoint group, so the load balancer sends traffic straight to pod IP addresses, and only to pods that pass the health check on slash health.

29
00:03:10,649 --> 00:03:16,573
The pod generates the answer from a model held in memory, and the response comes back the same way.

30
00:03:16,573 --> 00:03:22,849
Here's a real request: model v2 answered correctly, at about 17 tokens per second.

31
00:03:24,470 --> 00:03:25,969
Now the import path.

32
00:03:25,969 --> 00:03:30,071
The nodes have no internet access, so nothing is pulled at runtime.

33
00:03:30,071 --> 00:03:39,602
A Cloud Build job, running as its own service account, downloaded the llama.cpp server image, the k6 image, and both model files.

34
00:03:39,602 --> 00:03:44,133
It pushed the images to Artifact Registry and recorded their digests.

35
00:03:44,133 --> 00:03:48,560
The deployment references the server image by digest, not by tag.

36
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:57,089
Each model file was checked against the SHA-256 hash published by Hugging Face before upload, so a mismatch fails the build.

37
00:03:57,089 --> 00:04:07,011
When a pod starts, the GCS FUSE sidecar mounts the bucket read-only, authenticated as the pod's Kubernetes service account through Workload Identity.

38
00:04:08,618 --> 00:04:10,963
The security design follows from that.

39
00:04:10,963 --> 00:04:19,166
Nodes have private IP addresses only, and with no NAT there's no egress; you can see no external IPs and no routers.

40
00:04:19,166 --> 00:04:22,812
The control plane accepts connections from one IP address.

41
00:04:22,812 --> 00:04:28,958
The node service account has only the default node role, plus read access to one repository.

42
00:04:28,958 --> 00:04:36,406
The model server's Kubernetes service account can read one bucket, granted directly to its Workload Identity principal.

43
00:04:36,406 --> 00:04:40,026
There's no Google service account and no key file anywhere.

44
00:04:40,026 --> 00:04:49,049
The namespace enforces the restricted Pod Security standard: non-root, a read-only root filesystem, and all capabilities dropped.

45
00:04:49,049 --> 00:04:58,737
The gaps: the gateway is plain HTTP, with no authentication and no Cloud Armor, plus the default VPC and the Editor grant I mentioned.

46
00:05:00,374 --> 00:05:05,949
The main experiment was swapping the model from v1 to v2 while k6 sends load.

47
00:05:05,949 --> 00:05:13,123
Eight runs, two thousand and thirty-one requests, and five failures, all connection EOFs.

48
00:05:13,123 --> 00:05:22,029
They didn't follow the obvious pattern: a run without a pre-stop hook had zero failures, while a run with a 45-second pre-stop had two.

49
00:05:22,029 --> 00:05:30,350
My very first runs also had a test bug: the autoscaler wasn't actually pinned, so capacity changed in the middle of the test.

50
00:05:30,350 --> 00:05:32,225
I fixed that and re-ran.

51
00:05:33,842 --> 00:05:43,232
Load tests couldn't explain it, so I ran a targeted experiment: send one long generation to a pod, then delete that pod a few seconds in.

52
00:05:43,232 --> 00:05:46,812
With no pre-stop hook, the request never completed.

53
00:05:46,812 --> 00:05:55,992
It hung for 85 seconds, which is the 90-second grace period minus the four seconds before deletion, until the container was killed.

54
00:05:55,992 --> 00:06:03,193
With a 45-second pre-stop, the same request returned all 400 tokens in about 22 seconds.

55
00:06:03,193 --> 00:06:08,609
So llama.cpp stops generating on SIGTERM but leaves the connection open.

56
00:06:08,609 --> 00:06:13,805
The pre-stop hook has to outlast endpoint removal plus the longest generation.

57
00:06:15,422 --> 00:06:19,851
That still didn't explain the two failures with a 45-second pre-stop.

58
00:06:19,851 --> 00:06:21,153
The pod events did.

59
00:06:21,153 --> 00:06:27,260
Both failures happened at the exact second the old pod was killed, 90 seconds after deletion.

60
00:06:27,260 --> 00:06:32,286
So it was still receiving new requests 45 seconds after leaving the Service.

61
00:06:32,286 --> 00:06:35,710
Removing an endpoint only affects new connections.

62
00:06:35,710 --> 00:06:42,312
k6 reuses keep-alive connections, and connection tracking kept sending them to the termiNATing pod.

63
00:06:42,312 --> 00:06:48,210
With connection reuse turned off, the next run had zero failures in 95 requests.

64
00:06:48,210 --> 00:06:51,882
One run supports the explaNATion, but it doesn't prove it.

65
00:06:51,882 --> 00:07:00,098
The real fix is something that drains existing connections, such as a proxy in front of llama.cpp, or the load balancer path.

66
00:07:00,098 --> 00:07:03,223
So I can't claim zero-downtime model swaps yet.

67
00:07:04,850 --> 00:07:05,672
Scaling.

68
00:07:05,672 --> 00:07:15,268
With twelve concurrent users, CPU reached 87 percent of the pod's request within 22 seconds, and the autoscaler asked for a third pod.

69
00:07:15,268 --> 00:07:23,042
That pod was ready about 96 seconds later, most of it spent loading the 1.1-gigabyte model from Cloud Storage.

70
00:07:23,042 --> 00:07:39,253
CPU then settled at 63 percent, inside the autoscaler's tolerance, so scaling stopped, even though p95 latency had risen to about 12 seconds, roughly two and a half to four and a half times the earlier four-user runs.

71
00:07:39,253 --> 00:07:42,091
All 486 requests succeeded.

72
00:07:42,091 --> 00:07:48,237
Earlier in the day, the cluster autoscaler added spot nodes from one to four and hit its maximum.

73
00:07:48,237 --> 00:07:54,891
The lesson: CPU is a poor scaling signal for LLM serving, and queue depth is a better one.

74
00:07:56,510 --> 00:07:57,449
What I learned.

75
00:07:57,449 --> 00:08:01,720
Graceful shutdown is the server's job, not just Kubernetes'.

76
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:07,631
Size the pre-stop hook to the workload, which for LLMs means the longest generation.

77
00:08:07,631 --> 00:08:11,928
When load tests don't add up, run a small deterministic experiment.

78
00:08:11,928 --> 00:08:13,933
Check your test's own conditions.

79
00:08:13,933 --> 00:08:19,337
Pin images by digest: llama.cpp renamed a flag between builds.

80
00:08:19,337 --> 00:08:25,874
And read every Terraform plan: I re-applied with auto-approve, and it replaced the whole cluster.

81
00:08:25,874 --> 00:08:30,066
That mistake, and every other one, is in the troubleshooting log.

82
00:08:31,706 --> 00:08:39,924
Not covered yet: GPU serving, queue-depth autoscaling, blue-green routing, CI/CD, and TLS.

83
00:08:39,924 --> 00:08:46,968
The Terraform, the manifests, the test scripts, the raw results, and the full log are in the repository.

84
00:08:46,968 --> 00:08:48,114
Thanks for watching.
